\title{Information-criterion presets for sparse GLMs}
\description{
Both [L0glm.fit()] and [L0nnpois()] accept a numeric \code{lambda} or a
case-insensitive criterion name: \code{"aic"}, \code{"bic"}, \code{"gic"},
\code{"mebic"}, \code{"ebic"}, or \code{"mbic"}.
For example, \code{lambda = "GIC"} and \code{lambda = "gic"} are equivalent.
[L0glm.tune()] accepts the same names through its \code{tune} argument,
or \code{tune = "cv"} for cross-validation by held-out deviance.
}
\details{
For a single response, the target information criterion has the form
\deqn{-2\ell(\widehat\beta) + \lambda k,}
where \eqn{\ell} is the log-likelihood and \eqn{k} counts selected
coefficients. These presets set the per-coefficient \eqn{L_0} penalty on
the \emph{minus twice log-likelihood} scale, rather than on the
\eqn{-\ell} or mean-loss scale. All logarithms are natural.
In the fitters, \eqn{n} is the number of rows of \code{X} and \eqn{p} is its
number of columns after adding an intercept if requested. The preset is
resolved before any predictors are removed during fitting.

\describe{
  \item{\code{"aic"}: Akaike information criterion}{
    \eqn{\lambda = 2}. A prediction-oriented choice based on estimated
    expected Kullback--Leibler loss (Akaike, 1974). It often retains more
    variables and is not generally consistent for exact support recovery.}
  \item{\code{"bic"}: Bayesian information criterion}{
    \eqn{\lambda = \log n}. A conventional choice for identifying a
    parsimonious model when the candidate dimension is fixed or moderate
    (Schwarz, 1978). Its classical consistency guarantee requires a
    correctly specified identifiable model and regularity conditions;
    it need not control overfitting in very large model spaces.}
  \item{\code{"gic"}: generalized information criterion}{
    \eqn{\lambda = \log p\,\log\log n}. A selection-oriented choice for
    sparse problems, including high-dimensional settings. Fan and Tang
    (2013), Corollary 1, justify this choice under their assumptions on
    the design, response tails, signal strength and candidate model path.
    It is a consistent choice under those conditions, not a uniquely
    optimal penalty for every finite sample.}
  \item{\code{"mebic"}: modified extended BIC in Zhang (2024)}{
    \eqn{\lambda = 2\log p\,\log\log n}, exactly twice the GIC penalty.
    Use it when stronger complexity penalization is desired: it favors
    smaller models in exact criterion minimization, with a possible loss
    of sensitivity to weak signals. The factor of two remains at every
    sample size; the two criteria can still select the same true model
    asymptotically when both selectors are consistent.}
  \item{\code{"ebic"}: linearized extended BIC}{
    \eqn{\lambda = \log n + 2\gamma_{\rm EBIC}\log p}, with package
    default \eqn{\gamma_{\rm EBIC}=\max\{0,1-\log n/(2\log p)\}}.
    This accounts for large model spaces (Chen and Chen, 2008). The
    implementation replaces their combinatorial term
    \eqn{2\gamma_{\rm EBIC}\log {p \choose k}} by
    \eqn{2\gamma_{\rm EBIC}k\log p}, giving a constant penalty per
    coefficient. With this default it reduces to BIC when
    \eqn{p\le\sqrt n}. Supply a numeric \code{lambda} to use a different
    \eqn{\gamma_{\rm EBIC}}; it is separate from the fitter's adaptive-ridge
    exponent \code{gamma}.}
  \item{\code{"mbic"}: modified BIC}{
    \eqn{\lambda = \log(np^2/16)}. This multiplicity-adjusted criterion
    is intended for sparse selection among many candidate predictors
    (Bogdan, Ghosh and Zak-Szatkowska, 2008). The constant used here follows
    Frommlet and Nuel (2016). It is distinct from both MEBIC and the
    false-discovery-rate-oriented mBIC2 criterion.}
}
}
\section{GIC, SIC and HBIC}{

Zhu et al. (2020), in the ABESS paper, call the
\eqn{\log p\,\log\log n} choice the \emph{special information criterion}
(SIC) and introduce it as a special case of HBIC (Wang, Kim and Li, 2013).
The HBIC family uses \eqn{\lambda=C_n\log p}, with a slowly diverging
\eqn{C_n}; choosing \eqn{C_n=\log\log n} gives the same per-coefficient
penalty as Fan and Tang's GIC. For their
Gaussian least-squares problem,
\deqn{\mathrm{SIC}(S)=n\log\{\mathrm{RSS}_S/(2n)\}
      + |S|\log p\,\log\log n.}
This is the Gaussian profile-likelihood criterion up to an additive
constant; the factor \eqn{1/2} inside the logarithm does not halve the
complexity penalty. Use \code{"gic"} in this package; SIC and HBIC are
discussed here as literature terminology, not additional argument values.
}

\section{Approximation and scoring}{

Presets specify a target penalty; adaptive ridge does not guarantee a
global minimizer of the associated information criterion. The internal
ridge scaling \eqn{\widetilde\lambda=\lambda\phi/4}, with dispersion
\eqn{\phi=1} for Poisson and binomial models, follows the orthogonal
Gaussian threshold calibration of Frommlet and Nuel (2016). Applying this
calibration to general GLMs is an approximation. Criterion consistency
also does not by itself establish consistency of the numerical algorithm.
\code{relaxed = TRUE} refits selected coefficients with negligible
penalization; [L0glm.tune()] searches nearby penalties and evaluates the
requested criterion on the resulting fits.

Reported scores use the fitted likelihood and the package's parameter
counts, including nonzero intercepts and an extra dispersion parameter
where applicable. The metrics helper also augments \eqn{p} by one for
Gaussian, Gamma and inverse-Gaussian families. For multichannel fits,
reported IC scores count nonzero coefficient entries, not active rows;
the scalar presets do not establish a group-selection consistency result.
Likelihood-based criteria are not defined by the standard R quasi-family
likelihoods, so their reported scores can be \code{NA}.
}

\examples{
set.seed(7)
X <- matrix(runif(120), nrow = 40, ncol = 3)
y <- rpois(40, 1 + 3 * X[, 1])
fit <- L0glm.fit(X, y, family = poisson(identity),
  nonnegative = TRUE, intercept = TRUE, lambda = "MEBIC",
  parallelize = FALSE)
fast <- L0nnpois(X, y, intercept = TRUE, lambda = "mebic",
  parallelize = FALSE)
# Both presets use p = 4, including the added intercept.
c(fit$init.lambda, fast$init.lambda, 2 * log(4) * log(log(40)))

}
